%erg 0.1
Title: erg migrate discards the rollback provenance erg init preserves
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-16T12:42Z claude created

--- body ---

## Context

Found by ticket 0278's integration pass, in the rollback fixture the pass did
not originally have (see 0297). Measured on the real binary, not read off the
source.

Ticket 0279 gave `installAssets` an exemption whose whole purpose is to keep
evidence alive: when a run preserves an asset *because this binary predates the
stamp*, it returns early and does NOT rewrite `.erg-assets`. Its own comment
says why -- stamping with the older binary's rev/date "would record a state that
never happened, and would destroy the very evidence that said so, making the
next run misreport the same rollback as a local edit."

`erg migrate` calls the same function with `refuseDiverged=false`
(`migrate.go:263`). That leg never takes the preserve branch, so
`rollbackPreserved` is never set, so the early return never fires -- and the
manifest is rewritten. The exemption is scoped to the branch that raises the
flag, not to the condition the flag is about.

## Measured

Store: both managed assets diverged from embedded, `.erg-assets` recording the
on-disk hashes and dated `2099-01-01T00:00:00Z` (so the running binary predates
the stamp).

    $ erg check tickets/
      WARN .ergrc: embedded version is older than the .erg-assets stamp ...
      WARN AGENTS.md: embedded version is older than the .erg-assets stamp ...

    $ erg init .
    init: tickets/.ergrc is newer than this binary -- preserving ...
    init: tickets/AGENTS.md is newer than this binary -- preserving ...
    # .erg-assets untouched -- 0279's exemption, working as designed

    $ erg migrate tickets/
    init: downgraded tickets/AGENTS.md (git restore -- tickets/AGENTS.md to undo)
    # .erg-assets now: rev <this binary>, date <now>, EMBEDDED hashes for BOTH

    $ erg check tickets/
    ERG CHECK: PASS (1 tickets)      <-- silent

## What is and is not wrong here

The AGENTS.md overwrite is correct and announced -- charter decision 0224, and
`downgraded` is the right word for it. That part is not the defect.

Two things are:

1. `.ergrc` was never touched by this run (it is outside `migrateAssetPaths`)
   and still holds its divergence, yet the new manifest records it at the
   EMBEDDED hash. The manifest asserts a state that never happened. This is the
   same shape as ticket 0292's "stamps it as if shipped", reached by a different
   path.
2. The rollback condition goes silent afterwards, with no command having said
   anything about it. No file is lost -- a later `erg init` still preserves the
   diverged `.ergrc` as a local edit -- but the direction is gone, so the store
   can no longer tell "this binary is behind" from "someone edited this", and
   `erg init`'s remedy line changes accordingly.

It is the diagnostic half of ticket 0278's invariant: nothing was overwritten
silently, but the evidence that an overwrite WOULD be a revert was.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/init.go` -- `installAssets`: `rollbackPreserved` is set only inside
  the `preserve` branch; the manifest rewrite at the end is gated on it
- `src/go/migrate.go:263` -- the `refuseDiverged=false` call
- `src/go/manifest.go` -- `buildManifest` always writes `initAssetPaths`, so a
  migrate run stamps `.ergrc` too, an asset it never looks at
- `tests/test_assetinvariant.sh` -- arm 4 documents this in a comment and
  deliberately does not assert it

## Actions

1. Decide the rule. Two candidates, and the choice is a design call:
   (a) suppress the manifest rewrite whenever the store is in a rollback state,
   not only when a preserve fired -- `rollback` is already computed at the top
   of `installAssets`; or
   (b) stamp only the assets this run actually wrote, leaving other entries as
   they were, which also answers 0292's case and is the larger change.
2. Implement, with the comment saying which rule and why.

## Test

Red step: the rollback fixture above, `erg migrate tickets/`, then assert
`erg check` still reports the rollback WARN. It does not today.

Second arm: after that migrate, `.erg-assets` must not claim `.ergrc` is at the
embedded hash while the divergence is on disk.

## Verification

- [ ] rollback WARN survives `erg migrate`
- [ ] no manifest entry records a hash for an asset this run did not write
- [ ] `make check` green, including `tests/test_assetinvariant.sh`

## Invariants

- migrate must keep force-overwriting a diverged `AGENTS.md`, and keep calling
  it `downgraded` when the stamp is newer (charter 0224; two committed tests,
  plus arm 4 of `tests/test_assetinvariant.sh`)
- 0279's existing exemption on the init path must keep working

## Exit criteria

- A migrate run no longer erases the provenance that establishes a rollback
- The chosen rule is stated in the code, not only in this ticket

## Related

- 0279 -- introduced the exemption this path escapes
- 0292 -- the same "stamped as if shipped" defect on the init/stampless path;
  its action (b) above would cover both
- 0278 -- the integration pass that found this
- 0297 -- the suite arm that exercises the rollback state
